{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PWtZfph74c1bt6dUdnfUg7","type":"tool_result","content":"206\t[00:18:03] Selin Aydın: Okay, statement week. The cut-off date change.\n207\t[00:18:07] Kerem Yalçın: This is the one with the limit question.\n208\t[00:18:09] Selin Aydın: Yes. So in the IVR today... Burcu, you tell.\n209\t[00:18:13] Burcu Tan: In the IVR today the customer can change the statement week once a month, and at most three times in a year. Those are the error prompts we have, \"ayda bir\", \"yılda üç\". You have them in my prompt sheet.\n210\t[00:18:24] Kerem Yalçın: That rule changes with this project. From the product side we decided: once per calendar year. One change per year, per card.\n211\t[00:18:33] Burcu Tan: Since when?\n212\t[00:18:34] Kerem Yalçın: It's decided for the assistant channel. The IVR prompts will follow, I'll tell your team, but for this flow: once a calendar year.\n213\t[00:18:42] Burcu Tan: Okay. Then I need to change my prompts too. Noted.\n214\t[00:18:45] Deniz Arı: Calendar year meaning January to December? Not twelve months from the last change?\n215\t[00:18:50] Kerem Yalçın: Calendar year. January first resets.\n216\t[00:18:53] Deniz Arı: Okay. Then the important thing for us: if the customer already used it this year, we should not offer it.\n217\t[00:18:59] Kerem Yalçın: Right. Never offer it to someone who can't do it. That's the worst experience, you offer, they say yes, and then you say \"sorry you can't\".\n218\t[00:19:07] Ozan Er: But we need to know that before the offer. Is there a service that tells us if the right is used? In the contract Mert sent, I didn't see a field for it.\n219\t[00:19:15] Selin Aydın: Hm. I don't think there is one now.\n220\t[00:19:18] Kerem Yalçın: Let me check with Mert. It should be a small thing to add, a pre-check. Put it as an action on me.\n221\t[00:19:24] Deniz Arı: Okay. Kerem, pre-check for the yearly right.\n222\t[00:19:27] Ozan Er: And should the assistant tell the customer about the limit? Like before asking the date. \"Hesap kesim tarihinizi yılda bir kez değiştirebilirsiniz.\" Something like that.\n223\t[00:19:35] Kerem Yalçın: It can. I wouldn't make it a must.\n224\t[00:19:38] Elçin Ural: From compliance, there's no obligation to say it in the call. It's in the card agreement. If it helps the customer decide, fine, it's a good sentence.\n225\t[00:19:46] Burcu Tan: I'd say it, people will call back angry otherwise when they want to change again in March [laughs].\n226\t[00:19:51] Kerem Yalçın: Then let it be a nice-to-have. Ozan decides where it fits. The rule that matters is: one change per calendar year, and if the right is used, we don't offer the change at all.\n227\t[00:20:00] Ozan Er: Okay. And if the customer asks for it anyway, even though we didn't offer?\n228\t[00:20:04] Kerem Yalçın: Then explain that the change was already made this year. That's where that sentence is useful.\n229\t[00:20:09] Ozan Er: Okay, good.\n230\t[00:20:11] Selin Aydın: Next, joint statement. Elçin, this is also yours a bit.\n231\t[00:20:15] Elçin Ural: Linked cards?\n232\t[00:20:16] Selin Aydın: Yes. If two cards share one statement, a main card and a linked card, changing the week of one changes the other too. In my document, the assistant says it after the customer confirms. Like, \"okay, changed, and by the way this also affected your other card\".\n233\t[00:20:31] Elçin Ural: No. That's too late. The customer has to know before they say yes.\n234\t[00:20:35] Kerem Yalçın: Yes, that's not okay, they confirmed without knowing.\n235\t[00:20:38] Selin Aydın: Yes, I realised it when I reread it.\n236\t[00:20:41] Deniz Arı: So it goes before the confirm question. Before \"Onaylıyor musunuz?\".\n237\t[00:20:45] Elçin Ural: Before the confirm question, yes. Clearly.\n238\t[00:20:48] Ozan Er: Sentence? We had a draft, um, \"Bu değişiklik kartınıza bağlı [bağlı kart adı] kartınızı da etkileyecek.\"\n239\t[00:20:55] Elçin Ural: With the name of the linked card in it?\n240\t[00:20:57] Ozan Er: Yes, the card name comes from the service.\n241\t[00:21:00] Elçin Ural: Good. That's clear.\n242\t[00:21:02] Selin Aydın: \"Bu değişiklik kartınıza bağlı [bağlı kart adı] kartınızı da etkileyecek.\" Okay. And the other way, if the customer is calling about the linked card?\n243\t[00:21:11] Deniz Arı: Same idea. Before the confirm question we say the change also affects the main card.\n244\t[00:21:16] Kerem Yalçın: Yes. Whoever calls, before they confirm they hear that the other card moves too.\n245\t[00:21:21] Elçin Ural: And they have to say a clear yes. Not \"hmm okay maybe\".\n246\t[00:21:25] Deniz Arı: Yes, we only make the change after a clear yes. If it's unclear we ask once more, clearly.\n247\t[00:21:30] Elçin Ural: Good.\n248\t[00:21:32] Burcu Tan: Sorry, small thing, the IVR... [inaudible] ...the linked card holder, can they even change it? In the IVR only the main card holder can.\n249\t[00:21:40] Kerem Yalçın: In the assistant both can, if they own the card. That was in the scope.\n250\t[00:21:44] Burcu Tan: Okay. Just checking.\n251\t[00:21:46] Selin Aydın: Okay. Let me see what's left. E-mail. Not much, we did it in the mails. Only the registered address, read it back before sending.\n252\t[00:21:54] Deniz Arı: Yes. And one detail: if there's no registered e-mail at all, we don't offer the e-mail. We only offer the week change, if the customer has the right.\n253\t[00:22:02] Kerem Yalçın: Yes, logical.\n254\t[00:22:04] Ozan Er: And complex addresses, we shorten. Like \"m ile başlayan Gmail adresiniz\". We don't spell letter by letter.\n255\t[00:22:10] Elçin Ural: As long as it's the registered one, I'm fine."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"abc9990b-2e37-4da6-87b0-3f0fa959dc15","uuid":"79228341-4afd-4544-b4f6-26d6bfc0ac66","timestamp":"2026-10-11T17:55:28.931Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Burak\\Desktop\\Projects\\agent-builder\\.claude\\worktrees\\final-case\\.builder\\agentic\\final\\docs\\14-sources_meetings_2026-10-07-analysis-session-transcript.txt.txt","content":"[00:18:03] Selin Aydın: Okay, statement week. The cut-off date change.\n[00:18:07] Kerem Yalçın: This is the one with the limit question.\n[00:18:09] Selin Aydın: Yes. So in the IVR today... Burcu, you tell.\n[00:18:13] Burcu Tan: In the IVR today the customer can change the statement week once a month, and at most three times in a year. Those are the error prompts we have, \"ayda bir\", \"yılda üç\". You have them in my prompt sheet.\n[00:18:24] Kerem Yalçın: That rule changes with this project. From the product side we decided: once per calendar year. One change per year, per card.\n[00:18:33] Burcu Tan: Since when?\n[00:18:34] Kerem Yalçın: It's decided for the assistant channel. The IVR prompts will follow, I'll tell your team, but for this flow: once a calendar year.\n[00:18:42] Burcu Tan: Okay. Then I need to change my prompts too. Noted.\n[00:18:45] Deniz Arı: Calendar year meaning January to December? Not twelve months from the last change?\n[00:18:50] Kerem Yalçın: Calendar year. January first resets.\n[00:18:53] Deniz Arı: Okay. Then the important thing for us: if the customer already used it this year, we should not offer it.\n[00:18:59] Kerem Yalçın: Right. Never offer it to someone who can't do it. That's the worst experience, you offer, they say yes, and then you say \"sorry you can't\".\n[00:19:07] Ozan Er: But we need to know that before the offer. Is there a service that tells us if the right is used? In the contract Mert sent, I didn't see a field for it.\n[00:19:15] Selin Aydın: Hm. I don't think there is one now.\n[00:19:18] Kerem Yalçın: Let me check with Mert. It should be a small thing to add, a pre-check. Put it as an action on me.\n[00:19:24] Deniz Arı: Okay. Kerem, pre-check for the yearly right.\n[00:19:27] Ozan Er: And should the assistant tell the customer about the limit? Like before asking the date. \"Hesap kesim tarihinizi yılda bir kez değiştirebilirsiniz.\" Something like that.\n[00:19:35] Kerem Yalçın: It can. I wouldn't make it a must.\n[00:19:38] Elçin Ural: From compliance, there's no obligation to say it in the call. It's in the card agreement. If it helps the customer decide, fine, it's a good sentence.\n[00:19:46] Burcu Tan: I'd say it, people will call back angry otherwise when they want to change again in March [laughs].\n[00:19:51] Kerem Yalçın: Then let it be a nice-to-have. Ozan decides where it fits. The rule that matters is: one change per calendar year, and if the right is used, we don't offer the change at all.\n[00:20:00] Ozan Er: Okay. And if the customer asks for it anyway, even though we didn't offer?\n[00:20:04] Kerem Yalçın: Then explain that the change was already made this year. That's where that sentence is useful.\n[00:20:09] Ozan Er: Okay, good.\n[00:20:11] Selin Aydın: Next, joint statement. Elçin, this is also yours a bit.\n[00:20:15] Elçin Ural: Linked cards?\n[00:20:16] Selin Aydın: Yes. If two cards share one statement, a main card and a linked card, changing the week of one changes the other too. In my document, the assistant says it after the customer confirms. Like, \"okay, changed, and by the way this also affected your other card\".\n[00:20:31] Elçin Ural: No. That's too late. The customer has to know before they say yes.\n[00:20:35] Kerem Yalçın: Yes, that's not okay, they confirmed without knowing.\n[00:20:38] Selin Aydın: Yes, I realised it when I reread it.\n[00:20:41] Deniz Arı: So it goes before the confirm question. Before \"Onaylıyor musunuz?\".\n[00:20:45] Elçin Ural: Before the confirm question, yes. Clearly.\n[00:20:48] Ozan Er: Sentence? We had a draft, um, \"Bu değişiklik kartınıza bağlı [bağlı kart adı] kartınızı da etkileyecek.\"\n[00:20:55] Elçin Ural: With the name of the linked card in it?\n[00:20:57] Ozan Er: Yes, the card name comes from the service.\n[00:21:00] Elçin Ural: Good. That's clear.\n[00:21:02] Selin Aydın: \"Bu değişiklik kartınıza bağlı [bağlı kart adı] kartınızı da etkileyecek.\" Okay. And the other way, if the customer is calling about the linked card?\n[00:21:11] Deniz Arı: Same idea. Before the confirm question we say the change also affects the main card.\n[00:21:16] Kerem Yalçın: Yes. Whoever calls, before they confirm they hear that the other card moves too.\n[00:21:21] Elçin Ural: And they have to say a clear yes. Not \"hmm okay maybe\".\n[00:21:25] Deniz Arı: Yes, we only make the change after a clear yes. If it's unclear we ask once more, clearly.\n[00:21:30] Elçin Ural: Good.\n[00:21:32] Burcu Tan: Sorry, small thing, the IVR... [inaudible] ...the linked card holder, can they even change it? In the IVR only the main card holder can.\n[00:21:40] Kerem Yalçın: In the assistant both can, if they own the card. That was in the scope.\n[00:21:44] Burcu Tan: Okay. Just checking.\n[00:21:46] Selin Aydın: Okay. Let me see what's left. E-mail. Not much, we did it in the mails. Only the registered address, read it back before sending.\n[00:21:54] Deniz Arı: Yes. And one detail: if there's no registered e-mail at all, we don't offer the e-mail. We only offer the week change, if the customer has the right.\n[00:22:02] Kerem Yalçın: Yes, logical.\n[00:22:04] Ozan Er: And complex addresses, we shorten. Like \"m ile başlayan Gmail adresiniz\". We don't spell letter by letter.\n[00:22:10] Elçin Ural: As long as it's the registered one, I'm fine.","numLines":50,"startLine":206,"totalLines":323}}}
